      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py open lai_calibration_v3_23_16 --hypothesis \"Codify Lead Activity Index \\(LAI\\) calibration based on this session's empirical finding \\(5 named leads × 289 votes × 0 objections\\). Proposed: \\(a\\) auto-flag rule = any lead with objection_count==0 AND claims_voted>=10; \\(b\\) R88 LAR mandates min-1-dissent-per-30-votes for an engaged lead; \\(c\\) drop decoration_score threshold from 0.50 to 0.20; \\(d\\) PreToolUse hook reads LAI on cascade close, blocks ship if any active lead is flagged. Arithmetic context: under binomial with p_object=0.05, P\\(0 objections in 289 votes\\) ≈ 4.6e-7 — vanishingly unlikely under genuine engagement assumption.\" --falsification \"Falsified if: \\(a\\) the same lead consultation pattern run against MULTIPLE prior sessions shows nonzero objection rates — then this session is anomalous, not the leads; \\(b\\) selection bias proven: I only consult leads AFTER I've already justified a path, so they see no dissent-worthy material — then the fix is in WHEN leads are consulted, not min-dissent quotas; \\(c\\) R88 LAR creates perverse incentive — leads manufacture fake objections to hit quota. Each of these would change the proposal materially.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead EvidenceLead objection --note \"OBJECTION: N=70 claims from ONE session \\(session 78\\) is not a distribution — it is a single point. The 'decorative leads' hypothesis requires cross-session evidence. ASK: run LAI against sadp/_state/claim_history.jsonl entries from sessions 70-77 BEFORE codifying R88. If prior sessions show same 0-objection pattern, hypothesis strengthens; if prior sessions show variation, this session is anomalous \\(e.g., I may have been on a streak of well-evidenced claims\\). The 4.6e-7 binomial probability calculation assumes claims-are-i.i.d.-binomial, which is wrong — claims have STRUCTURE \\(cascade-close items have low contention; novel hypotheses have high contention\\). Recommend: stratify by claim TYPE \\(cascade-mechanical vs novel-hypothesis\\) before computing objection-rate priors. DECISION: do NOT codify R88 thresholds until cross-session + stratified-by-claim-type data captured.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead CorrectnessLead objection --note \"OBJECTION: hypothesis 'leads are decorative' has an unfalsified alternative: 'leads engage substantively but never had grounds to object because the agent never proposed dissent-worthy material.' These are observationally equivalent in the verdict-rate dimension. Need a POSITIVE TEST: deliberately surface a flawed claim \\(one with a known TYPE A/B/C error or arithmetic gap or unsupported generalization\\), consult leads, observe whether they catch it. If 5/5 clear a known-bad claim → decoration confirmed. If 1+/5 object → leads engage when given real material; root cause is in WHEN/HOW I consult them. R88 LAR would treat both cases identically; that's wrong. DECISION: run the positive-test experiment before any rule codification.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead ParityRootCauseLead objection --note \"ARITHMETIC OBJECTION: 4.6e-7 calculation assumes independent Bernoulli trials with p_object=0.05 baseline. This assumption is broken by SELECTION BIAS: I consult leads only AFTER I have already justified the path to myself. Leads see post-decision-rationalized arguments, not pre-decision uncertainty. Conditional on 'I have already justified the path,' P\\(lead finds flaw\\) is much lower than baseline 0.05 — maybe 0.005. Then P\\(0 in 289 | p=0.005\\) = 0.995^289 ≈ 0.235, which is NOT extreme. The arithmetic case for 'leads are decorative' DEPENDS on the p_object prior, which the analysis didn't establish. Gap closure: arithmetic shows 0 objections is consistent with EITHER decoration OR selection bias. Cannot distinguish from observed data alone. DECISION: insufficient arithmetic evidence to codify R88; need conditional probability framing or positive-test experiment.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead CSPhDArchetype objection --note \"OBJECTION on R88 LAR design: if the rule mandates min-1-dissent-per-30-votes, a rational agent \\(me, or a future lead implementation\\) could manufacture token objections to hit the quota. Then R88 produces FAKE engagement that looks real in the metrics while actual quality drops. Perverse-incentive failure mode = Goodhart's law applied to lead dissent. CAVEAT: the rule needs a quality check on dissent \\(concrete alternative + reasoning\\), not just count. Without quality gate, R88 trades one decoration problem \\(no objections\\) for another \\(token objections\\). DECISION: revise R88 design before codification — require dissent to include a falsification hypothesis, a citation, and a concrete next step the operator can take. If R88 cannot operationalize quality, defer codification.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead FeedbackVisibilityLead objection --note \"OBJECTION on LAI output format: aggregate per-lead metrics are useful but operator's real question is 'WHICH claim WHICH lead failed on.' Current LAI lacks per-claim drilldown. Without it, operator sees scores but cannot audit specific instances. Add: --drilldown CLI mode that emits, for each lead, a list of the N most-suspicious claims \\(high note-similarity to prior, low novelty, short note, clear verdict on a controversial topic\\). Also: LAI text mode showed accented chars as boxes due to console encoding — fix output to render cleanly on the operator's terminal. DECISION: do not ship LAI v1 to operator-facing surface until drilldown + encoding are in place.\")",
