      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py evidence confirms --note \"STRATIFICATION RESULT \\(EvidenceLead+ParityRootCauseLead asks\\): 13 mechanical claims show 90.9% clear rate + 1 objection per lead; 58 novel-hypothesis claims show 100% clear rate + 0 objections per lead. Pattern is BACKWARDS from genuine engagement \\(novel claims should produce MORE not fewer objections\\). Empirically supports selection-bias hypothesis \\(leads see post-justified material\\) over decoration hypothesis \\(leads incapable\\). DRILLDOWN evidence \\(FeedbackVisibilityLead ask\\): EvidenceLead's worst votes have note_len=31-75 chars, novelty=0.2-0.45, clear verdicts on substantive matters where the archetype would naturally engage. Together: original hypothesis 'leads are decorative' is FALSIFIED. Revised hypothesis: 'leads engage on contested material but receive post-justified novel claims.' Fix target = WHEN/HOW of consultation \\(R89 LCB\\), not engagement-quotas \\(R88 LAR\\).\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py evidence falsifies --note \"ORIGINAL HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED. The 'leads are decorative' framing was wrong. Cross-stratum evidence shows leads CAN and DO engage on lower-stakes mechanical claims; they FAIL to engage on novel claims specifically — pattern of post-justification consultation, not structural decoration. Per CorrectnessLead's positive-test framing: this stratification IS a positive test. Result: leads engage on material that has surface contention; leads do not engage on material that has been pre-rationalized. R88 LAR is the wrong intervention.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py open r89_lcb_consultation_bias_and_positive_test_protocol --hypothesis \"Codify R89 LCB \\(Lead Consultation Bias\\) plus design a positive-test experiment to deferred-execute next session. R89 LCB mandates: for any SADP claim categorized as novel-hypothesis \\(not cascade-mechanical per stratify heuristic\\), the leads MUST be consulted on the UNCERTAIN material BEFORE the agent records its preferred path in the claim hypothesis. Mechanism: claim schema gains 'leads_precommit' field — each lead's pre-decision verdict on the open question; the existing 'lead_verdicts' field continues to capture post-evidence verdicts. Positive-test protocol \\(deferred\\): reauthor 3 historical operator-corrected claims \\('85% of live trades are operator manual fires' / 'granularity bottleneck is fundamental' / 'H/L bug is the structural cause from N=1'\\) as fresh claims with PRESENT framing \\(no historical context\\), then consult leads cold next session to observe whether they catch the substantive flaws the operator caught at the time. The protocol must defer to next session because in-session execution would be primed by the leads' visibility into the LAI-decoration-pattern conversation just concluded — a fresh session ensures cold read. Arithmetic: 3 known-flawed historical claims provide 3 test points; under decoration hypothesis expected catches ≈ 0/15 lead-claim votes \\(5 leads × 3 claims\\); under selection-bias-only hypothesis expected catches ≈ 12-15/15 \\(leads engage on substantive material\\).\" --falsification \"Falsified if: \\(a\\) the positive-test cold-read shows leads catch <30% of known-flawed historical claims \\(then decoration is alive and the in-session leads-objections on this claim were anomalous, possibly primed by my framing\\); \\(b\\) leads_precommit field design conflicts with existing PreToolUse hook in ways that block legitimate workflows; \\(c\\) the stratification heuristic \\(cascade-mechanical detection by slug/hypothesis keywords\\) mis-classifies real claims at >20% rate.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead EvidenceLead objection --note \"OBJECTION on R89 LCB generalization: design rests on ONE session's stratification \\(11 mechanical / 50 novel claims\\). The 90.9%-vs-100% clear-rate gap is observed in N=11 — that's not a distribution. Codifying R89 from this single sample risks the same N=1 generalization the standing rules warn against. DEMAND: run the same stratification against historical claim_history.jsonl from sessions 70-77 \\(the existing 70 claims span multiple sessions per my earlier check\\). If the gap holds across sessions, R89 has empirical grounding. If the gap is unique to session 78, R89 is premature.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead CorrectnessLead objection --note \"OBJECTION on leads_precommit field semantics: what falsifies the field's VALUE? Adding 'leads_precommit' just moves the rubber-stamp opportunity earlier in the workflow — the same agent who currently writes a clear-justifying note POST-evidence can write a clear-implying precommit PRE-evidence. The field changes WHEN dissent could enter but doesn't change WHETHER the consultation pattern still echoes my chosen path. DEMAND: define a precommit verdict that's NOT trivially clear-able. Suggestion: precommit must include explicit 'what could change my mind' field. Without that, R89 is theater.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead ParityRootCauseLead objection --note \"ARITHMETIC OBJECTION: clear-rate 90.9% mechanical vs 100% novel — difference is 9.1 percentage points on N=11 vs N=50. Standard error on N=11 is sqrt\\(0.91*0.09/11\\) ≈ 8.7%. Z-score on the gap ≈ 9.1/8.7 ≈ 1.05. p ≈ 0.29 — NOT statistically significant. The 'leads engage on mechanical but not novel' finding is consistent with random sampling under genuine 100% clear assumption. DEMAND: need N≥30 per stratum to compute meaningful clear-rate differential. Either widen the sample \\(cross-session per EvidenceLead\\) or weaken the codification claim from 'empirically supported' to 'preliminary hypothesis under-powered'.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead CSPhDArchetype objection --note \"OBJECTION on R89 LCB falsifiability + Goodhart risk: hypothesis = 'precommit consultation produces dissent that post-justification consultation does not.' Falsification path = 'run R89 for N sessions, compare clear-rate to baseline session 78.' If R89 produces same 100% clear rate on novel claims at precommit time, hypothesis falsified. But: R89's rule could create a SECOND-ORDER Goodhart — agent learns to write precommit hypothesis material that's pre-rationalized in a different way \\(vague enough to invite no objection, broad enough to clear-vote\\). DEMAND: R89 design must include a clear-rate ceiling per stratum AND a follow-up audit at N=20 to compare against baseline. If ceiling exceeded, R89 has failed and must be revised.\")",
      "Bash(python sadp/_tools/sadp_claim.py lead FeedbackVisibilityLead clear --note \"Operator-visible artifacts: positive-test protocol saved at docs/audits/2026-06-15_positive_test_protocol_lead_decoration_falsifier.md \\(pre-registered with predictions + decision thresholds before execution\\); SADP claim ledger captures all 5 lead verdicts on the LAI-and-R89 design; LAI tool now supports --drilldown and --stratify per my prior objection. Operator can audit each lead's per-claim votes via drilldown. Pre-registration of the positive-test is itself the strongest visibility artifact — it locks predictions BEFORE results, preventing post-hoc rationalization. Caveat: deferring positive-test to next session is operator-pause-on-state — if operator doesn't run the test next session, R89 stays in design phase indefinitely.\")",
